From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Rosenberg <drosen@google.com>
Date: Mon, 1 Jul 2024 14:56:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fuse: let Android's MediaProvider use passthrough

Upstream FUSE passthrough cannot serve Android's shared storage, which
MediaProvider serves as a FUSE daemon without CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Upstream
refuses to register backing files without that capability, turns
passthrough off when the server also asks for the writeback cache, and
makes the io modes of an inode exclusive, whereas MediaProvider opens
one file in both modes at once (an open that must redact location Exif
stays on the daemon, the same file opened by another app is passed
through).

The Android common kernel carries the changes that lift this. They are
combined here behind the new CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID, which only
the Android product enables, so a desktop kernel compiles them out:

- Allow passthrough without CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Android's SELinux policy
  keeps every app but MediaProvider off /dev/fuse, and of the domains
  that may issue ioctls on it only MediaProvider and system_server run
  without CAP_SYS_ADMIN. The AppFuse of system_server never negotiates
  passthrough, which a connection needs to register a backing file.
- Allow a passthrough and a cached open of one inode, and count the
  passthrough opens apart (iopassctr, IOM_PASSTHROUGH).
- Allow passthrough with the writeback cache (a revert of upstream
  3ab394b363c5) and keep the attributes consistent: a passthrough write
  takes them from the backing file.
- Keep the cache coherent: flush the dirty pages before a passthrough
  read, invalidate the written range after a passthrough write, and
  allow a cached mmap next to passthrough opens.
- Add /sys/fs/fuse/features/fuse_passthrough, by which MediaProvider
  finds that the kernel does all of this.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Rosenberg <drosen@google.com>
Signed-off-by: Carlos Llamas <cmllamas@google.com>
(squash of the Android common kernel android17-6.18 commits
6fb1128f2700, 3ae410445fb1, 65687870ee84, d30885a29d65, 24955a554f92,
62c103092ea2 and 610f86469e66, all by Daniel Rosenberg; their test is
atest android.scopedstorage.cts.general.ScopedStorageDeviceTest)
[Adapted to 6.18.60: every change is keyed on the new
CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID. Two things differ from the Android
kernel. Registering a backing file still requires a connection that
negotiated passthrough, which the Android kernel dropped. A passthrough
write and splice_write flush the dirty pages of the inode first, as the
read side does: a cached writer's older page is then not written back
over the write, and the size the write takes from the backing file does
not drop the cached pages beyond it. The Android kernel's quirks in
fuse_inode_uncached_io_end() and fuse_is_io_cache_wait() are kept, and
MediaProvider cannot reach them, as it never asks for parallel direct
writes: when a parallel direct write ends, the backing file of the
inode is dropped although passthrough opens remain, and a cached open
that arrives during a parallel direct write beside a passthrough open
does not wait for it.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-fs-fuse |  8 +++
 fs/fuse/Kconfig                         | 22 +++++++
 fs/fuse/backing.c                       | 16 ++++-
 fs/fuse/file.c                          | 15 ++++-
 fs/fuse/fuse_i.h                        | 16 ++++-
 fs/fuse/inode.c                         | 47 ++++++++++++++-
 fs/fuse/iomode.c                        | 77 +++++++++++++++++++++++--
 fs/fuse/passthrough.c                   | 54 ++++++++++++++++-
 8 files changed, 242 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-fs-fuse

diff --git a/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-fs-fuse b/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-fs-fuse
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..1535a14fa6dec8387a8b9e17cf3d85950c06bb69
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-fs-fuse
@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+What:		/sys/fs/fuse/features/fuse_passthrough
+Date:		February 2025
+Contact:	Daniel Rosenberg <drosen@google.com>
+Description:
+		Read-only file that contains the word 'supported' if fuse
+		passthrough with Android modifications is supported, does not
+		exist otherwise. The modifications are those of
+		CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID.
diff --git a/fs/fuse/Kconfig b/fs/fuse/Kconfig
index 23572fcafdd16a176ccf8bc659fe32b9b744e185..531276b3b59fe2d556f33f75904d57127d6d7fd7 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/Kconfig
+++ b/fs/fuse/Kconfig
@@ -83,6 +83,28 @@ config FUSE_CANONICAL_PATH
 
 	  Say N unless the guest is Android.
 
+config FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	bool "FUSE passthrough as Android's MediaProvider uses it"
+	depends on FUSE_PASSTHROUGH
+	help
+	  This lets a FUSE server without CAP_SYS_ADMIN register backing
+	  files, keeps passthrough on when the server also asks for the
+	  writeback cache, and lets one inode have passthrough and cached
+	  opens at the same time. Upstream refuses all three.
+
+	  Android's MediaProvider serves shared storage with all three, and
+	  tells that the kernel provides them by the file
+	  /sys/fs/fuse/features/fuse_passthrough, which this option adds.
+
+	  Registering a backing file then needs no CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Android's
+	  SELinux policy keeps every app but MediaProvider off /dev/fuse, and
+	  of the domains that may issue ioctls on it only MediaProvider and
+	  system_server run without CAP_SYS_ADMIN. The AppFuse of system_server
+	  never negotiates passthrough, which a connection needs to register a
+	  backing file.
+
+	  Say N unless the guest is Android.
+
 config FUSE_IO_URING
 	bool "FUSE communication over io-uring"
 	default y
diff --git a/fs/fuse/backing.c b/fs/fuse/backing.c
index 4afda419dd14168ea1b2ec49f2e7f2bcb87d7774..0aa639d0f20ba31170bd3994664a4741f5240c4b 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/backing.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/backing.c
@@ -89,8 +89,18 @@ int fuse_backing_open(struct fuse_conn *fc, struct fuse_backing_map *map)
 	pr_debug("%s: fd=%d flags=0x%x\n", __func__, map->fd, map->flags);
 
 	/* TODO: relax CAP_SYS_ADMIN once backing files are visible to lsof */
+	/*
+	 * Android's SELinux policy keeps every app but the media provider off
+	 * /dev/fuse, and of the domains that may issue ioctls on it only the
+	 * media provider and system_server run without CAP_SYS_ADMIN. The
+	 * AppFuse of system_server never negotiates passthrough, which the
+	 * check below refuses, and the media provider is not to be given
+	 * CAP_SYS_ADMIN.
+	 */
 	res = -EPERM;
-	if (!fc->passthrough || !capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN))
+	if (!fc->passthrough ||
+	    (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) &&
+	     !capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)))
 		goto out;
 
 	res = -EINVAL;
@@ -146,7 +156,9 @@ int fuse_backing_close(struct fuse_conn *fc, int backing_id)
 
 	/* TODO: relax CAP_SYS_ADMIN once backing files are visible to lsof */
 	err = -EPERM;
-	if (!fc->passthrough || !capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN))
+	if (!fc->passthrough ||
+	    (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) &&
+	     !capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)))
 		goto out;
 
 	err = -EINVAL;
diff --git a/fs/fuse/file.c b/fs/fuse/file.c
index 0812eee881519c119b62ea21b2c20615be1d03e0..87a471340cc9b23ab33725d88e6b94fb0ae91ea7 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/file.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/file.c
@@ -2341,10 +2341,20 @@ static int fuse_file_mmap(struct file *file, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 	 * If inode is in passthrough io mode, because it has some file open
 	 * in passthrough mode, either mmap to backing file or fail mmap,
 	 * because mixing cached mmap and passthrough io mode is not allowed.
+	 *
+	 * Android's MediaProvider opens one inode in both modes and maps both,
+	 * so there a cached mmap is allowed next to passthrough opens. A shared
+	 * mapping of both kinds is not coherent between them. A passthrough
+	 * read or write first flushes the dirty pages of the cached opens and
+	 * a passthrough write then invalidates the range it wrote, so it sees
+	 * the cached writes that completed before it and the cached opens see
+	 * its write afterwards. A cached write racing with a passthrough write
+	 * has no order.
 	 */
 	if (fuse_file_passthrough(ff))
 		return fuse_passthrough_mmap(file, vma);
-	else if (fuse_inode_backing(get_fuse_inode(inode)))
+	else if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) &&
+		 fuse_inode_backing(get_fuse_inode(inode)))
 		return -ENODEV;
 
 	/*
@@ -3169,6 +3179,9 @@ void fuse_init_file_inode(struct inode *inode, unsigned int flags)
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fi->queued_writes);
 	fi->writectr = 0;
 	fi->iocachectr = 0;
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	fi->iopassctr = 0;
+#endif
 	init_waitqueue_head(&fi->page_waitq);
 	init_waitqueue_head(&fi->direct_io_waitq);
 
diff --git a/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h b/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
index 56e0019ca7b492e4830c7776b595d67fd022498d..4bc85f34314aee228217daf6f2bedf37c25585da 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
+++ b/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
@@ -157,6 +157,11 @@ struct fuse_inode {
 			/** Number of files/maps using page cache */
 			int iocachectr;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+			/* Number of files using passthrough */
+			int iopassctr;
+#endif
+
 			/* Waitq for writepage completion */
 			wait_queue_head_t page_waitq;
 
@@ -284,8 +289,15 @@ struct fuse_file {
 	/** Wait queue head for poll */
 	wait_queue_head_t poll_wait;
 
-	/** Does file hold a fi->iocachectr refcount? */
-	enum { IOM_NONE, IOM_CACHED, IOM_UNCACHED } iomode;
+	/** Does file hold a fi->iocachectr (or fi->iopassctr) refcount? */
+	enum {
+		IOM_NONE,
+		IOM_CACHED,
+		IOM_UNCACHED,
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+		IOM_PASSTHROUGH,
+#endif
+	} iomode;
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH
 	/** Reference to backing file in passthrough mode */
diff --git a/fs/fuse/inode.c b/fs/fuse/inode.c
index 091a375c32a8dcaba370fa58884092feb341975a..43ae515522f08d79151822f71e233f6c6f95121b 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/inode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/inode.c
@@ -193,6 +193,9 @@ static void fuse_evict_inode(struct inode *inode)
 	}
 	if (S_ISREG(inode->i_mode) && !fuse_is_bad(inode)) {
 		WARN_ON(fi->iocachectr != 0);
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+		WARN_ON(fi->iopassctr != 0);
+#endif
 		WARN_ON(!list_empty(&fi->write_files));
 		WARN_ON(!list_empty(&fi->queued_writes));
 	}
@@ -1430,13 +1433,15 @@ static void process_init_reply(struct fuse_args *args, int error)
 			 *
 			 * Also don't allow the combination of FUSE_PASSTHROUGH
 			 * and FUSE_WRITEBACK_CACHE, current design doesn't handle
-			 * them together.
+			 * them together, except for Android's MediaProvider,
+			 * which asks for both.
 			 */
 			if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH) &&
 			    (flags & FUSE_PASSTHROUGH) &&
 			    arg->max_stack_depth > 0 &&
 			    arg->max_stack_depth <= FILESYSTEM_MAX_STACK_DEPTH &&
-			    !(flags & FUSE_WRITEBACK_CACHE))  {
+			    (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) ||
+			     !(flags & FUSE_WRITEBACK_CACHE)))  {
 				fc->passthrough = 1;
 				fc->max_stack_depth = arg->max_stack_depth;
 				fm->sb->s_stack_depth = arg->max_stack_depth;
@@ -2244,6 +2249,32 @@ static void fuse_fs_cleanup(void)
 
 static struct kobject *fuse_kobj;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+static ssize_t fuse_passthrough_show(struct kobject *kobj,
+				     struct kobj_attribute *attr, char *buff)
+{
+	return sysfs_emit(buff, "supported\n");
+}
+
+static struct kobj_attribute fuse_passthrough_attr =
+		__ATTR_RO(fuse_passthrough);
+
+static struct attribute *fuse_features[] = {
+	&fuse_passthrough_attr.attr,
+	NULL,
+};
+
+static const struct attribute_group fuse_features_group = {
+	.name = "features",
+	.attrs = fuse_features,
+};
+
+static const struct attribute_group *attribute_groups[] = {
+	&fuse_features_group,
+	NULL
+};
+#endif
+
 static int fuse_sysfs_init(void)
 {
 	int err;
@@ -2258,8 +2289,17 @@ static int fuse_sysfs_init(void)
 	if (err)
 		goto out_fuse_unregister;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	err = sysfs_create_groups(fuse_kobj, attribute_groups);
+	if (err)
+		goto out_fuse_remove_mount_point;
+#endif
 	return 0;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+out_fuse_remove_mount_point:
+	sysfs_remove_mount_point(fuse_kobj, "connections");
+#endif
  out_fuse_unregister:
 	kobject_put(fuse_kobj);
  out_err:
@@ -2268,6 +2308,9 @@ static int fuse_sysfs_init(void)
 
 static void fuse_sysfs_cleanup(void)
 {
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	sysfs_remove_groups(fuse_kobj, attribute_groups);
+#endif
 	sysfs_remove_mount_point(fuse_kobj, "connections");
 	kobject_put(fuse_kobj);
 }
diff --git a/fs/fuse/iomode.c b/fs/fuse/iomode.c
index 3728933188f307a07e019e0d64c05735ebbbe934..869ce415f2c714a27888e868b9dd955188ccfad9 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/iomode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/iomode.c
@@ -50,14 +50,21 @@ int fuse_file_cached_io_open(struct inode *inode, struct fuse_file *ff)
 	/*
 	 * Check if inode entered passthrough io mode while waiting for parallel
 	 * dio write completion.
+	 *
+	 * Android's MediaProvider opens an inode in both modes at once (a
+	 * redacted open stays cached), so there it is no failure.
 	 */
-	if (fuse_inode_backing(fi)) {
+	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) &&
+	    fuse_inode_backing(fi)) {
 		clear_bit(FUSE_I_CACHE_IO_MODE, &fi->state);
 		spin_unlock(&fi->lock);
 		return -ETXTBSY;
 	}
 
 	WARN_ON(ff->iomode == IOM_UNCACHED);
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	WARN_ON(ff->iomode == IOM_PASSTHROUGH);
+#endif
 	if (ff->iomode == IOM_NONE) {
 		ff->iomode = IOM_CACHED;
 		if (fi->iocachectr == 0)
@@ -81,6 +88,15 @@ static void fuse_file_cached_io_release(struct fuse_file *ff,
 	spin_unlock(&fi->lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * On Android a passthrough open is counted apart (iopassctr) and excludes no
+ * cached open, which MediaProvider needs for the opens it must redact.
+ */
+static inline bool fuse_io_passthrough_apart(struct fuse_backing *fb)
+{
+	return IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) && fb;
+}
+
 /* Start strictly uncached io mode where cache access is not allowed */
 int fuse_inode_uncached_io_start(struct fuse_inode *fi, struct fuse_backing *fb)
 {
@@ -94,10 +110,15 @@ int fuse_inode_uncached_io_start(struct fuse_inode *fi, struct fuse_backing *fb)
 		err = -EBUSY;
 		goto unlock;
 	}
-	if (fi->iocachectr > 0) {
+	if (fi->iocachectr > 0 && !fuse_io_passthrough_apart(fb)) {
 		err = -ETXTBSY;
 		goto unlock;
 	}
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	if (fb)
+		fi->iopassctr++;
+	else
+#endif
 	fi->iocachectr--;
 
 	/* fuse inode holds a single refcount of backing file */
@@ -125,7 +146,11 @@ static int fuse_file_uncached_io_open(struct inode *inode,
 		return err;
 
 	WARN_ON(ff->iomode != IOM_NONE);
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	ff->iomode = IOM_PASSTHROUGH;
+#else
 	ff->iomode = IOM_UNCACHED;
+#endif
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -154,16 +179,50 @@ static void fuse_file_uncached_io_release(struct fuse_file *ff,
 	fuse_inode_uncached_io_end(fi);
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+static void fuse_inode_passthrough_io_end(struct fuse_inode *fi)
+{
+	struct fuse_backing *oldfb = NULL;
+
+	spin_lock(&fi->lock);
+	WARN_ON(fi->iopassctr == 0);
+	fi->iopassctr--;
+	if (!fi->iopassctr)
+		oldfb = fuse_inode_backing_set(fi, NULL);
+	spin_unlock(&fi->lock);
+	if (oldfb)
+		fuse_backing_put(oldfb);
+}
+
+/* Drop passthrough io reference from passthrough open */
+static void fuse_file_passthrough_io_release(struct fuse_file *ff,
+					     struct fuse_inode *fi)
+{
+	WARN_ON(ff->iomode != IOM_PASSTHROUGH);
+	ff->iomode = IOM_NONE;
+	fuse_inode_passthrough_io_end(fi);
+}
+#endif
+
 /*
  * Open flags that are allowed in combination with FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH.
  * A combination of FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH and FOPEN_DIRECT_IO means that read/write
  * operations go directly to the server, but mmap is done on the backing file.
  * FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH mode should not co-exist with any users of the fuse inode
  * page cache, so FOPEN_KEEP_CACHE is a strange and undesired combination.
+ *
+ * On Android it is the normal one: MediaProvider keeps the cache of a file that
+ * is open in both modes.
  */
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+#define FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID_MASK	FOPEN_KEEP_CACHE
+#else
+#define FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID_MASK	0
+#endif
+
 #define FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH_MASK \
 	(FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH | FOPEN_DIRECT_IO | FOPEN_PARALLEL_DIRECT_WRITES | \
-	 FOPEN_NOFLUSH)
+	 FOPEN_NOFLUSH | FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID_MASK)
 
 static int fuse_file_passthrough_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 {
@@ -208,10 +267,13 @@ int fuse_file_io_open(struct file *file, struct inode *inode)
 
 	/*
 	 * Server is expected to use FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH for all opens of an inode
-	 * which is already open for passthrough.
+	 * which is already open for passthrough, except on Android, where the
+	 * writeback cache allows both.
 	 */
 	err = -EINVAL;
-	if (fuse_inode_backing(fi) && !(ff->open_flags & FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH))
+	if (fuse_inode_backing(fi) && !(ff->open_flags & FOPEN_PASSTHROUGH) &&
+	    !(IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID) &&
+	      get_fuse_conn(inode)->writeback_cache))
 		goto fail;
 
 	/*
@@ -271,5 +333,10 @@ void fuse_file_io_release(struct fuse_file *ff, struct inode *inode)
 	case IOM_CACHED:
 		fuse_file_cached_io_release(ff, fi);
 		break;
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	case IOM_PASSTHROUGH:
+		fuse_file_passthrough_io_release(ff, fi);
+		break;
+#endif
 	}
 }
diff --git a/fs/fuse/passthrough.c b/fs/fuse/passthrough.c
index f2d08ac2459b7e05578edd56960e1028d6d1bb49..6f625cfc18111f35e4f1a59a9be5b1b5e1610130 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/passthrough.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/passthrough.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <linux/file.h>
 #include <linux/backing-file.h>
 #include <linux/splice.h>
+#include <linux/pagemap.h>
 
 static void fuse_file_accessed(struct file *file)
 {
@@ -22,7 +23,35 @@ static void fuse_passthrough_end_write(struct kiocb *iocb, ssize_t ret)
 {
 	struct inode *inode = file_inode(iocb->ki_filp);
 
-	fuse_write_update_attr(inode, iocb->ki_pos, ret);
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	if (get_fuse_conn(inode)->writeback_cache) {
+		/*
+		 * With the writeback cache the kernel, not the server, is the
+		 * arbiter of the attributes: fuse_write_update_attr() would
+		 * only invalidate them and the server's update would then be
+		 * ignored. Take them from the backing file instead.
+		 */
+		struct fuse_file *ff = iocb->ki_filp->private_data;
+		struct inode *backing_inode =
+			file_inode(fuse_file_passthrough(ff));
+
+		inode_set_mtime_to_ts(inode, inode_get_mtime(backing_inode));
+		inode_set_ctime_to_ts(inode, inode_get_ctime(backing_inode));
+		inode->i_blocks = backing_inode->i_blocks;
+		i_size_write(inode, i_size_read(backing_inode));
+	} else
+#endif
+	{
+		fuse_write_update_attr(inode, iocb->ki_pos, ret);
+	}
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	/* Pages cached by a cached open of the file are stale after a write */
+	if (ret > 0)
+		invalidate_inode_pages2_range(inode->i_mapping,
+					      (iocb->ki_pos - ret) >> PAGE_SHIFT,
+					      iocb->ki_pos >> PAGE_SHIFT);
+#endif
 }
 
 ssize_t fuse_passthrough_read_iter(struct kiocb *iocb, struct iov_iter *iter)
@@ -44,6 +73,13 @@ ssize_t fuse_passthrough_read_iter(struct kiocb *iocb, struct iov_iter *iter)
 	if (!count)
 		return 0;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	/*
+	 * A cached open of the file may have dirty pages that only the server
+	 * can write back, and the backing file would not show them.
+	 */
+	write_inode_now(file_inode(file), 1);
+#endif
 	ret = backing_file_read_iter(backing_file, iter, iocb, iocb->ki_flags,
 				     &ctx);
 
@@ -70,6 +106,14 @@ ssize_t fuse_passthrough_write_iter(struct kiocb *iocb,
 	if (!count)
 		return 0;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_FUSE_PASSTHROUGH_ANDROID
+	/*
+	 * A cached open of the file may have dirty pages. Written back after
+	 * this write they would overwrite it, and the size taken from the
+	 * backing file afterwards would drop those beyond its end.
+	 */
+	write_inode_now(inode, 1);
+#endif
 	inode_lock(inode);
 	ret = backing_file_write_iter(backing_file, iter, iocb, iocb->ki_flags,
